{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observation\nIn tests/unit/reconciler/orchestrate/test_mode_cap_enforcement.py, test_spot_check_sampling_is_deterministic_across_runs calls _fresh_renderer() twice. That helper calls importlib.import_module(\"rebar.reconciler.manifest_renderer\") with the same name in one Python process, so both calls return the cached module. The test then calls render_throttle twice and compares the nonempty spot_check keys. Its helper comment and assertion describe fresh module loads and per-process Python hash randomization, but the test never starts another process or changes PYTHONHASHSEED. This was found during the comment-remediation review of intangible-corruptful-numbat; the prose-only patch must leave this unlisted helper/assertion unchanged.\n\n## Verification (2026-10-24, current main)\n- `importlib.import_module` returns the cached module, so both `_fresh_renderer()` calls in `test_spot_check_sampling_is_deterministic_across_runs` return the same module object in one process, under one hash seed. The test therefore cannot fail if the sampler regresses to the built-in `hash()`.\n- The sampler (`src/rebar/reconciler/manifest_renderer.py`) selects keys with `_stable_bucket(target) == 0`, a SHA-256 bucket, which is why the cross-process result must be identical.\n- No test in the tree sets `PYTHONHASHSEED`.\n\n## Approach\n1. Add a cross-process test: run a short child interpreter twice, with `PYTHONHASHSEED` set to 1 and to 2, each rendering the same 200 target fixture through `render_throttle` and printing the ordered spot-check keys as JSON; assert both outputs are equal and non-empty. The child gets an explicit environment and uses the installed `rebar` package, so the test is hermetic.\n2. Give the harness teeth. The child is a short script passed to `python -c` with one argument, `real` or `builtin-hash`. For `builtin-hash` the script assigns `manifest_renderer._stable_bucket = lambda target: hash(target) % 10` inside the child, before it calls `render_throttle`, because a monkeypatch in the test process cannot reach a separately spawned interpreter. The test runs that variant under both seeds and asserts the keys DISAGREE. A harness that cannot show the defect would pass for free.\n3. Correct the existing in-process test: its docstring and assertion message claim cross-load and per-process-hash coverage that a same-process, same-seed comparison does not provide. State what it proves (repeated renders in one process agree and are non-empty) and point to the new test for cross-process stability.\n\n## Scope\nIn scope: `tests/unit/reconciler/orchestrate/test_mode_cap_enforcement.py`. Out of scope: any change to the renderer.\n\n## Testing\n```\npytest tests/unit/reconciler/orchestrate/test_mode_cap_enforcement.py -q\nmake verify\n```\nFailure case: against a copy of the renderer that uses `hash()`, the cross-process test fails and the in-process test still passes, which is the gap this ticket describes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Two independent processes with distinct `PYTHONHASHSEED` values render the same 200-target fixture to the same non-empty ordered spot-check keys (checked: new test).\n- [x] The same harness with a `hash()`-based bucket yields different keys across the two seeds (checked: seeded-defect test).\n- [x] The in-process test's docstring and message describe only what it observes (checked: diff review).\n- [x] `make verify` is green on the Linux CI gate (`Verified +1` on the Gerrit change).\n"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1792907511120783001,"uuid":"268c4963-e0e8-428d-b7bf-06f9d3f803d1"}